<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Debug builds only. The manifest merger adds this receiver to the debug variant; it is
  absent from release entirely, along with the class itself.

  It exists because SMS_RECEIVED is a protected broadcast that adb cannot send, and this
  machine has no emulator. See DebugSmsReceiver for the full reasoning.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <application>
        <!--
          ⚠ Exported with no permission, and lint is right to object in general — an
          unprotected exported receiver is something any app on the phone can trigger.

          Suppressed on this element only, never project-wide, for two reasons:

          1. It has to be reachable from `adb shell`, which runs as uid 2000 and holds no
             special permission. A permission requirement here would make it unusable for
             the only purpose it has.
          2. It does not exist in a release build. This file lives in src/debug, so neither
             the receiver nor its class is in the shipped APK.

          If Sika is ever published, check this is still true rather than trusting the
          comment.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".sms.DebugSmsReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            tools:ignore="ExportedReceiver" />

        <!--
          ⚠ **The same receiver as in the main manifest, exported for debug builds only.**
          `tools:replace` is what makes that legal: without it the merger sees exported="false"
          from src/main and exported="true" here and fails the build rather than guessing.

          It exists because the semester check runs at 9am and there is no way to ask it a
          question from `adb` otherwise — a broadcast from uid 2000 to an unexported receiver
          is accepted into the queue and then silently dropped, which reads exactly like the
          receiver running and finding nothing to say. That cost a wrong "it works" on
          2026-09-04.

          ⚠ **This proves the receiver and the notification, NOT the alarm delivering to it.**
          Same caveat as DebugSmsReceiver: a hand-sent broadcast is not the real trigger. The
          alarm path is identical to MonthlyReport's, which was proved on the emulator with a
          rolled clock on 2026-09-02.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notify.TermAlertReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            tools:ignore="ExportedReceiver"
            tools:replace="android:exported" />
    </application>

</manifest>
